"A DNS resolution diagram traces a query through resolver behavior, caches, delegation, and authoritative answers.\n\nUse when: Explain recursive versus iterative lookup. Investigate which authority or cache determines a name’s answer.\n\nAvoid when: Only final network reachability or application authorization matters.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish client stub, recursive resolver, and authoritative servers. Query and response arrows label names, record types, and relevant referrals. Cache annotations state observed TTL or hypothetical cache state explicitly.\n\nBuild: Choose the queried name, record type, and starting cache conditions. Draw client recursion separately from resolver iteration. Add delegations, aliases, and relevant negative answers. Route replies on distinct lanes and keep record data away from server labels.\n\nCheck: Authoritative and recursive roles are not conflated. Cached answers and referrals are distinguished. TTL is not presented as a guaranteed global propagation deadline. Query names and response labels remain unobscured by opposing arrows.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming every lookup contacts the root servers. Treating a DNS answer as proof that the destination service is reachable.\n\nExample: A resolver follows root and parent-zone referrals to an authoritative server, then returns and caches the requested A record.\n\nAlternatives: Logical network diagram, Sequence diagram."
